    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 5yr CAGR (7.2%) diverges >30% from EPS 3yr CAGR (23.5%) and revenue 3yr CAGR (2.8%). Revenue 5yr (10.3%) is inflated by the US Concrete (2021) acquisition; the 3yr EPS/FCF CAGRs start from a depressed FY2022 base (cost inflation). The forward base path uses ~5-6% organic growth (mid-single-digit aggregates pricing + ~0-1% volume), below the selected equity CAGR.",
    "notes": "Owner FCF = reported FCF - SBC - equity-award withholding taxes (no interest income strip; net debt). Owner-FCF CAGRs (29.7% 3yr, 9.7% 5yr) sit slightly above reported (28.5%, 9.9% -> 3yr owner above because FY22 base is small); SBC is ~1% of revenue so the adjustment is minor. FY2025 owner FCF $1,038.2M vs reported $1,135.3M."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Vulcan executes its pure-play aggregates strategy: FY2026 lands at the $2.5B adjusted-EBITDA midpoint despite energy inflation and wet Q2 weather, then freight-adjusted pricing compounds at ~4-5% (Q2-26: +5% mix-adjusted) with 0-2% volume growth supported by public construction and large projects. Cash gross profit per ton keeps rising (>$12 in Q2-26) and the exit from Houston asphalt and California ready-mix lifts the group margin, taking (EBITDA+SBC)/revenue from ~31.6% to ~34.5% by 2031. Capex runs slightly above D&A; 15-20% of revenue converts to unlevered owner FCF. Organic only - bolt-on acquisitions (e.g. the Brannan quarry and DFW rail yard) are treated as value-neutral at the price paid.",
    "bull": "The aggregates pricing cycle stays unusually strong: a federal surface-transportation reauthorisation at or above IIJA funding, a wave of data-centre, semiconductor, LNG and manufacturing megaprojects in VMC's Sun Belt footprint, and a residential recovery as mortgage rates ease together lift volumes 2-3%/yr on top of 5-6% price. Operating leverage on a fixed quarry cost base and continued SAG discipline (6.9% of revenue TTM) push the margin to ~37%. Terminal growth 3.5% reflects irreplaceable, permitted reserves near growing metros.",
    "bear": "IIJA expires on 30-Sep-2026 and is followed by a run of short-term extensions instead of a full reauthorisation, state DOT lettings slow, residential stays depressed and private non-residential megaprojects are deferred. Shipments fall high-single-digits across 2027-28 (as in 2008-10, though far less severe) while pricing still rises ~3%; fixed-cost deleverage plus diesel and liquid-asphalt inflation compress margins ~3pp before a slow recovery to ~31%. Capex is cut to defend cash. Terminal growth 2.5% and a 15x cap reflect a lower-multiple cyclical rerating."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Vulcan is a high-quality, locally-monopolistic aggregates franchise whose value lies in permitted reserves and pricing power, but it remains a construction cyclical. The house view charges SBC and equity-award withholding taxes in full, models organic growth only, charges the cycle once through a 25%-weighted downturn scenario rather than a WACC premium, and caps the terminal at 15-20x FCF. The market currently pays ~27x unlevered owner FCF, which this model only supports in the bull case. The price sits above even the street (SBC-as-non-cash) value in the base case, so band_position exceeds 100%: the gap to the price is a growth/multiple question, not an SBC-accounting one.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Federal highway funding: IIJA expires 30-Sep-2026; reauthorisation size and timing drive public volumes (the largest end market)",
      "Residential and private non-residential construction cyclicality (rates, megaproject timing)",
      "Energy (diesel, liquid asphalt) cost inflation compressing unit margins, as seen in Q2-26 (+7% unit cash cost)",
      "Weather volatility in Texas and the Southeast shifting volumes between periods",
      "M&A: growth via acquisitions is not modelled; overpaying would destroy value the model does not show",
      "Terminal multiple: 55-66% of enterprise value sits in the terminal (base 61%); each 1pp of terminal growth moves the base value materially"
    ]
  },
