    "notes": "MODEL: owner-FCF component DCF (routing: US operating company, cyclical construction materials) with the cyclical adaptations (risk charged once in the flows, lower terminal cap than a compounder). Not a REIT/bank/holdco. FY0 = FY2025 ($7,941.1M revenue, CSV EBITDA $2,368.1M). Anchors refreshed to the Q2-26 10-Q/earnings release (8-K ex99.1, 29-Jul-2026), which the Metrics CSV does not yet carry (it ends at Q1-26): total debt $4,364.3M, cash+restricted cash $288.7M, company-defined net debt $4,075.6M. GUIDANCE: FY2026 Adjusted EBITDA $2.4-2.6B reaffirmed (midpoint $2.5B; D&A ~$700M; interest ~$215M). VMC's Adjusted EBITDA does NOT add back SBC, so ebitda_margin_path here is (Adj EBITDA + SBC)/revenue to avoid charging SBC twice: FY26 ~ ($2,500 + ~$60)/~$8,100 = 31.6%. FY26 revenue growth is only ~2% because the Houston asphalt/construction business (sold Q4-25) and California ready-mix (sold Jun-26, $572M H1 divestiture proceeds) drop out of H2; H1-26 reported revenue was +4.7%. Growth paths are ORGANIC (price + volume); bolt-on M&A is not modelled, neither its cash cost nor its revenue. Working capital: adjusted NWC ~$945M on ~$8.1B revenue (~11.7%), so wc_capture is NEGATIVE (-12% of revenue increase). Interest income not stripped: VMC carries net debt. Buybacks recorded but value-neutral (flat count). Pension and asset-retirement obligations not added to the bridge (in the flows as accretion / contributions). Analysis JSON was not present at build time; scenario axes drawn from the filings and the Q2-26 release.",
